abstract: "We determine exactly which hypotheses suffer false confidence under flat-prior posteriors in location models with radially decreasing errors. For exponential-power errors with p>1, including the Gaussian case, a hypothesis is valid at every threshold at a parameter exactly when that parameter lies outside the interior of the closed convex hull of the essential support of the hypothesis. This equivalence holds for a radial density g with tail probability S if and only if liminf S(R)/g(R-b)=0 for every b>0. A hypothesis and its complement are both globally valid only for almost-halfspaces, which corrects a conjecture of Martin (2024) in these models, and only strictly monotone functions of one linear form have exactly calibrated marginal posteriors. With log-concave errors, a finite-level theorem shows that if the complement of a hypothesis fits in a closed convex set of posterior mass at most s<1/2 when the observation equals the truth, the hypothesis receives posterior probability at least 1-s with sampling probability at least one half. Unrefereed candidate; internal AI-assisted review only. Text CC0-1.0; code MIT."
